A||||1||mshill||3.4L pushrod on order, what else do I need?||||1||
Z||000000||mshill||10-04-2000||04:55 PM||||Ok, I broke down and have ordered a 3.4L pushrod engine (longblock, used w/less than 50K miles) and I need to know what else I need:<P>flywheel? (for a 4 speed)<BR>starter holes drilled.<BR>AC Bracket bolt welded on?<P>TerryK is bead blasting my plenum and valve covers so I can paint them.  My manifolds will be getting rewelded and ported.<P>I may or may not go with an adjustable fuel pressure regulator.<P>What else?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||88formula||10-04-2000||09:08 PM||||Make sure the check valve in the oil passage behind the filter is in the 3.4 engine. If it is not you must remove the one from your 2.8 and install it into your 3.4 engine. You will also have to drill out the threads in one of the bell housing holes, but I can't remember which one right now. If you compare it to your 2.8 block you will see what I mean. <P>To get the best performance from your 3.4 engine you will want to have a custom prom chip made for you but it is not absolutely necessary because the 3.4 will run fine on the stock chip. <P>Use the 3.4 injectors if you can but the 2.8 injectors will also work. The rest is a piece of cake and you will have about 165 horsepower @ 5000 rpm and 208 ft. lbs. of torque @ 3000 rpm with the stock motor in a fiero. That will give a 2925 lb. Fiero a 0-60 time of 6.4 seconds and a 1/4-mile time of 14.6 seconds @ 92 mph if you launch a 3500-rpm and shift at 6000 rpm with a 3.65 final drive. That is a whole lot better than the stock 2.8 performance of 0-60 in 7.9 seconds and the 1/4-mile in 15.7 seconds. Your gas mileage will be about the same as a stock 2.8.<BR>||||reg||1
